1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,360
Two rescues.

2
00:00:01,680 --> 00:00:04,480
Every Cavendish banana plant is a clone, and

3
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:07,600
the fungus TR4 can kill any of them,

4
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:10,560
so Australian scientists added a resistance gene from

5
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:11,600
a wild banana.

6
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:14,160
And the northern white rhino is down to

7
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:17,680
two females, so scientists are making rhino embryos

8
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:18,640
in the lab.

9
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:21,760
Both use genetic techniques to stop something dying

10
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:22,440
out.

11
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:25,680
But does each one actually increase biodiversity?

12
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:28,400
That depends on which level you look at,

13
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:29,920
and what you compare it with.

14
00:00:31,134 --> 00:00:33,373
Biodiversity isn't one number.

15
00:00:33,694 --> 00:00:34,973
It has three levels.

16
00:00:35,534 --> 00:00:38,253
Genetic diversity is the variety of alleles in

17
00:00:38,294 --> 00:00:41,293
a population or species: its gene pool.

18
00:00:41,934 --> 00:00:44,893
Species diversity is the variety of species living

19
00:00:44,974 --> 00:00:45,693
in an area.

20
00:00:46,174 --> 00:00:49,373
And ecosystem diversity is the variety of habitats

21
00:00:49,414 --> 00:00:51,613
and communities across a whole region.

22
00:00:52,174 --> 00:00:54,333
And a technique can raise one level and

23
00:00:54,414 --> 00:00:57,613
lower another, so you trace each one separately.

24
00:00:58,834 --> 00:01:01,953
There's a second question: compared with what?

25
00:01:02,274 --> 00:01:04,833
An effect is a change from something, and

26
00:01:04,914 --> 00:01:07,033
that something is your baseline.

27
00:01:07,314 --> 00:01:09,873
A modified crop field might hold more insect

28
00:01:10,154 --> 00:01:13,073
species than a sprayed conventional field, but far

29
00:01:13,234 --> 00:01:14,833
fewer than the bush it replaced.

30
00:01:15,314 --> 00:01:17,313
Same field, opposite answers.

31
00:01:17,714 --> 00:01:20,113
So name your baseline before you judge.

32
00:01:21,334 --> 00:01:22,453
Back to the bananas.

33
00:01:23,014 --> 00:01:26,053
Cavendish plants are grown from suckers, so every

34
00:01:26,214 --> 00:01:28,853
plant is a copy, with the same alleles.

35
00:01:29,414 --> 00:01:33,893
The modified banana, QCAV-4, carries a resistance gene

36
00:01:33,974 --> 00:01:36,533
from a wild banana, and in field trials

37
00:01:36,774 --> 00:01:38,373
it resisted TR4.

38
00:01:38,934 --> 00:01:40,693
It was approved for growing in Australia in

39
00:01:40,774 --> 00:01:44,133
2024, as a back-up if TR4 spreads.

40
00:01:44,774 --> 00:01:47,333
But at the genetic level, it's still one

41
00:01:47,414 --> 00:01:47,973
clone.

42
00:01:48,454 --> 00:01:51,333
Compared with ordinary Cavendish, it adds one useful

43
00:01:51,414 --> 00:01:51,813
gene.

44
00:01:52,294 --> 00:01:55,093
Compared with growing many varieties, the crop's gene

45
00:01:55,174 --> 00:01:56,933
pool stays very narrow.

46
00:01:58,134 --> 00:01:59,573
Now the field itself.

47
00:02:00,054 --> 00:02:03,573
With far fewer insecticide sprays, Bt cotton can

48
00:02:03,654 --> 00:02:06,853
leave more insect species alive than sprayed conventional

49
00:02:06,934 --> 00:02:09,813
cotton: a gain at the species level, on

50
00:02:09,894 --> 00:02:10,613
that baseline.

51
00:02:11,174 --> 00:02:13,813
Crops modified to tolerate a weedkiller can work

52
00:02:13,854 --> 00:02:14,533
the other way.

53
00:02:15,014 --> 00:02:17,973
Farmers can spray the whole crop, so compared

54
00:02:18,054 --> 00:02:21,333
with fields where some weeds survive, fewer wildflowers

55
00:02:21,414 --> 00:02:23,253
are left for insects to feed on.

56
00:02:23,574 --> 00:02:26,453
And at the ecosystem level, higher yields could

57
00:02:26,534 --> 00:02:29,413
mean less bush cleared for farming, but only

58
00:02:29,494 --> 00:02:31,573
if that land is actually protected.

59
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:33,840
Now the rhino.

60
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:36,560
The last male died in 2018.

61
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:40,480
The two remaining females, Najin and Fatu, can't

62
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:41,600
carry a pregnancy.

63
00:02:42,240 --> 00:02:45,680
So scientists collect eggs from Fatu, fertilise them

64
00:02:45,720 --> 00:02:47,840
in the lab with sperm frozen from males

65
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:50,400
that have died, and freeze the embryos.

66
00:02:50,960 --> 00:02:53,680
They've made dozens, and placed some into southern

67
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:56,960
white rhinos, a close relative, as surrogate mothers.

68
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:00,560
But no northern white pregnancy has lasted yet.

69
00:03:01,767 --> 00:03:03,246
Trace the levels.

70
00:03:03,527 --> 00:03:06,486
This could preserve the northern white rhino subspecies

71
00:03:06,647 --> 00:03:08,886
and some of its genetic diversity.

72
00:03:09,207 --> 00:03:10,966
It would add a species to a particular

73
00:03:11,047 --> 00:03:13,766
area only if white rhinos are absent from

74
00:03:13,847 --> 00:03:14,966
that baseline area.

75
00:03:15,527 --> 00:03:17,926
But at the genetic level, every calf would

76
00:03:18,007 --> 00:03:20,326
come from one female's eggs and a few

77
00:03:20,407 --> 00:03:21,366
males' sperm.

78
00:03:21,847 --> 00:03:24,486
That's a tiny gene pool, a bottleneck from

79
00:03:24,527 --> 00:03:25,206
the start.

80
00:03:25,847 --> 00:03:29,046
So a second route matters: turning frozen skin

81
00:03:29,127 --> 00:03:31,686
cells from about a dozen rhinos into stem

82
00:03:31,767 --> 00:03:34,966
cells, then into eggs and sperm, could widen

83
00:03:35,047 --> 00:03:35,206
it.

84
00:03:35,847 --> 00:03:39,526
And the ecosystem gain, rhinos grazing African grasslands

85
00:03:39,687 --> 00:03:42,086
again, is decades away at best.

86
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:46,180
Notice what isn't a level: yield.

87
00:03:46,660 --> 00:03:48,540
A crop that grows more food is a

88
00:03:48,580 --> 00:03:51,540
production gain, not a biodiversity gain.

89
00:03:52,020 --> 00:03:54,420
And saving a subspecies isn't a gain at

90
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:55,220
every level.

91
00:03:55,860 --> 00:03:57,780
Strong answers say it like this:

92
00:03:58,340 --> 00:04:01,220
this could preserve the northern white rhino subspecies

93
00:04:01,380 --> 00:04:03,540
and some of its genetic diversity.

94
00:04:03,780 --> 00:04:05,540
It would add a species to a particular

95
00:04:05,620 --> 00:04:08,260
area only if white rhinos are absent from

96
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:09,460
that baseline area.

97
00:04:10,020 --> 00:04:13,380
Then judge the bottleneck and the ecosystem separately.

98
00:04:13,860 --> 00:04:16,820
And keep the scale in mind: on land,

99
00:04:16,940 --> 00:04:20,020
clearing habitat, mostly for farming, is the biggest

100
00:04:20,100 --> 00:04:22,100
driver of biodiversity loss.

101
00:04:22,340 --> 00:04:24,740
That happens whatever the crop, so to judge

102
00:04:24,780 --> 00:04:27,940
the technique itself, compare a modified crop with

103
00:04:28,020 --> 00:04:29,620
the farming it replaces.

104
00:04:30,834 --> 00:04:32,353
Two things to hold on to.

105
00:04:32,914 --> 00:04:36,433
The banana problem returns: any technique that copies

106
00:04:36,474 --> 00:04:40,273
the best individual, whether cloning, one popular variety

107
00:04:40,354 --> 00:04:43,553
or one top sire, narrows the gene pool,

108
00:04:43,674 --> 00:04:45,713
so one disease can hit many plants or

109
00:04:45,794 --> 00:04:47,113
animals at once.

110
00:04:47,474 --> 00:04:50,993
And for every effect, ask: which level, and

111
00:04:51,234 --> 00:04:52,433
compared with what?

112
00:04:53,634 --> 00:04:55,153
Two exam questions now.

113
00:04:55,634 --> 00:04:56,313
First.

114
00:04:56,594 --> 00:04:58,913
A crop has been modified to tolerate a

115
00:04:58,994 --> 00:04:59,713
weedkiller.

116
00:05:00,194 --> 00:05:02,513
It grows beside a small population of a

117
00:05:02,674 --> 00:05:05,073
wild relative that can cross with it, and

118
00:05:05,154 --> 00:05:07,873
farmers spray the weedkiller along the field edges

119
00:05:07,954 --> 00:05:09,553
where the wild plants grow.

120
00:05:10,194 --> 00:05:13,073
Explain how this could reduce the genetic diversity

121
00:05:13,154 --> 00:05:14,513
of the wild population.

122
00:05:15,154 --> 00:05:16,433
Pause here to answer.

123
00:05:26,834 --> 00:05:29,473
Pollen carries the tolerance gene into the wild

124
00:05:29,634 --> 00:05:32,033
plants: that's gene flow.

125
00:05:32,354 --> 00:05:35,313
Where the weedkiller is sprayed, only wild plants

126
00:05:35,394 --> 00:05:38,433
carrying it survive and breed, so selection makes

127
00:05:38,514 --> 00:05:41,313
it common, and their alleles replace many local

128
00:05:41,474 --> 00:05:42,433
ones.

129
00:05:42,674 --> 00:05:45,953
Repeated crossing can also swamp the small population,

130
00:05:46,034 --> 00:05:48,273
diluting its local alleles.

131
00:05:48,314 --> 00:05:50,353
Saying a crop's genes can't reach a different

132
00:05:50,714 --> 00:05:52,753
population is the classic mistake.

133
00:05:53,967 --> 00:05:54,526
Second.

134
00:05:55,007 --> 00:05:57,886
In the early twentieth century, a fungal disease

135
00:05:57,967 --> 00:06:00,526
killed billions of American chestnut trees.

136
00:06:01,247 --> 00:06:04,526
Scientists propose planting chestnuts modified with a gene

137
00:06:04,607 --> 00:06:07,646
that resists the fungus, crossed with surviving wild

138
00:06:07,727 --> 00:06:08,366
chestnuts.

139
00:06:09,087 --> 00:06:12,446
Evaluate the claim that this will improve biodiversity.

140
00:06:12,767 --> 00:06:14,206
Pause here to answer.

141
00:06:24,634 --> 00:06:28,073
Species level: a once-common tree could return.

142
00:06:28,714 --> 00:06:32,153
Genetic level: crossing with many wild survivors keeps

143
00:06:32,234 --> 00:06:34,873
their alleles; a few clones wouldn't.

144
00:06:35,194 --> 00:06:38,713
Ecosystem level: animals could regain a food tree,

145
00:06:38,834 --> 00:06:41,033
but other effects are uncertain.

146
00:06:41,114 --> 00:06:43,833
So the claim is partly supported: a likely

147
00:06:43,914 --> 00:06:47,033
species gain, a genetic gain only if bred

148
00:06:47,114 --> 00:06:48,073
widely.

149
00:06:48,114 --> 00:06:50,793
Saying it saves a species, so every level

150
00:06:50,914 --> 00:06:53,193
improves, is the classic mistake.

151
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:57,040
So when a question asks how a biotechnology

152
00:06:57,120 --> 00:07:01,120
changes biodiversity, name the case and the baseline.

153
00:07:01,360 --> 00:07:04,240
Then trace each level: alleles in the gene

154
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:07,560
pool, species in the area, habitats across the

155
00:07:07,600 --> 00:07:08,360
region.

156
00:07:08,640 --> 00:07:11,400
Say which way each one moves, and why.

157
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:14,080
Next, how to weigh those effects against the

158
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:14,880
benefits.

159
00:07:16,100 --> 00:07:17,540
Here are the key notes.

160
00:07:17,780 --> 00:07:19,700
Pause the video if you need more time

161
00:07:19,780 --> 00:07:20,580
to copy them.
